# Loge: a guided tour

```elixir
root = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)

Mix.install([
  {:loge, path: Path.join(root, "loge"), override: true},
  {:loge_tptp, path: Path.join(root, "loge_tptp")},
  {:loge_dimacs, path: Path.join(root, "loge_dimacs")},
  {:loge_asp, path: Path.join(root, "loge_asp")},
  {:loge_prolog, path: Path.join(root, "loge_prolog")},
  {:loge_egglog, path: Path.join(root, "loge_egglog")},
  {:loge_smtlib, path: Path.join(root, "loge_smtlib")},
  {:kino_loge, path: Path.join(root, "kino_loge")}
])
```

## What Loge is

Loge ("logic engines") lets a notebook write problems in several logic languages and
hand them to provers and solvers. Each language is its own package; this notebook
installs all of them:

| Package       | Language               | Engines                                          |
| ------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `loge_tptp`   | TPTP                   | SystemOnTPTP, StarExec, a local prover, Isabelle |
| `loge_dimacs` | DIMACS CNF             | clasp                                            |
| `loge_asp`    | Answer Set Programming | clingo                                           |
| `loge_prolog` | Prolog                 | SWI-Prolog                                       |
| `loge_egglog` | egglog                 | egglog                                           |
| `loge_smtlib` | SMT-LIB 2.6            | z3, cvc5                                         |

Every problem below sits in a **Logic Engine** smart cell (`+ Smart` → *Logic Engine*).
From top to bottom, a cell has:

1. a **language** and an **engine** select;
2. the engine's **controls**, such as a time limit;
3. the **editor**, and under it the problems the linter finds as you type;
4. once evaluated, the outcome: a **Result** tab with the verdict and the details the
   engine reports, and an **Output** tab with what the engine printed.

Evaluating a cell runs ordinary Elixir. Open a cell's source (the `<>` toggle) to see
it: one `Loge.run!/3` call, which works without the smart cell installed.

## Settings

Some engines need settings that hold across cells: a server, a password, the path to
a binary. The **Logic Engine Settings** cell holds them, one engine at a time, and
**Verify** checks that the engine can be reached. A password field takes the name of
a Livebook secret, never the password itself.

None of the engines in this tour need settings if their programs are on `PATH`:
SystemOnTPTP is a public web service; clingo and SWI-Prolog come bundled; Isabelle,
z3 and cvc5 are found on `PATH`. If z3 lives elsewhere, set its executable here.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJ2YWx1ZXMiOnt9fQ","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.ConfigCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
:ok
```

## Proving theorems: TPTP

TPTP is the language of automated theorem provers. A cell with no engine and a
variable name is a **definitions** cell: it holds axioms that later cells include by
that name. This one defines groups:

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
groups =
  Loge.source(
    :tptp,
    "fof(associativity, axiom, ![X, Y, Z]: mult(mult(X, Y), Z) = mult(X, mult(Y, Z))).\nfof(left_identity, axiom, ![X]: mult(e, X) = X).\nfof(left_inverse, axiom, ![X]: mult(inv(X), X) = e).\n"
  )
```

A classic exercise: in a group where every element is its own inverse, multiplication
commutes. The cell includes `groups` and asks the E prover on SystemOnTPTP.
Expected: **Theorem**. If you edit the definitions above, this cell becomes stale, as
any cell that reads a variable does.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Tptp.Engines.SystemOnTptp,
  "include('groups').\n\nfof(self_inverse, hypothesis, ![X]: mult(X, X) = e).\nfof(commutative, conjecture, ![X, Y]: mult(X, Y) = mult(Y, X)).\n",
  system: "E",
  time_limit_sec: 10,
  includes: %{"groups" => groups}
)
```

Without that hypothesis, commutativity does not follow. Vampire can tell, and
expected is **CounterSatisfiable** (or **GaveUp** within the time limit). The SZS
status is the standard vocabulary of these verdicts; the Result tab links to its
definitions.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJlbmdpbmUiOiJzeXN0ZW1fb25fdHB0cCIsImxhbmd1YWdlIjoidHB0cCIsIm9wdGlvbnMiOnsic3lzdGVtIjoiVmFtcGlyZSIsInRpbWVfbGltaXRfc2VjIjoxMH0sInNvdXJjZSI6ImluY2x1ZGUoJ2dyb3VwcycpLlxuXG5mb2YoY29tbXV0YXRpdmUsIGNvbmplY3R1cmUsICFbWCwgWV06IG11bHQoWCwgWSkgPSBtdWx0KFksIFgpKS4iLCJ2YXJpYWJsZSI6IiJ9","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Tptp.Engines.SystemOnTptp,
  "include('groups').\n\nfof(commutative, conjecture, ![X, Y]: mult(X, Y) = mult(Y, X)).",
  system: "Vampire",
  time_limit_sec: 10,
  includes: %{"groups" => groups}
)
```

Higher-order problems (THF) can also go to Isabelle. With `isabelle` on `PATH` and no
server configured, Loge starts a local Isabelle server on first use, about six seconds
for the first proof, and stops it with the notebook's runtime. Each formula becomes a
lemma; the table lists their statuses. Expected: **Theorem** for both.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Tptp.Engines.Isabelle,
  "thf(excluded_middle, conjecture, ![P: $o]: (P | ~ P)).\nthf(drinker, conjecture, ?[X: $i]: ![Y: $i]: ((d @ X) => (d @ Y))).\nthf(d_type, type, d: $i > $o).\n",
  proof_method: "by auto"
)
```

## Colouring a map, three ways

Can the seven regions of Australia be coloured with three colours so that no two
neighbours share one? The same puzzle reads differently in each language.

In **ASP**, a choice rule picks one colour per region and a constraint rules out equal
neighbours. clingo returns the stable models. Expected: **Satisfiable**, one painting.
Set *Models* to `0` to see all 18.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Asp.Engines.Clingo,
  "region(wa; nt; sa; q; nsw; v; t).\nborder(wa, nt). border(wa, sa). border(nt, sa). border(nt, q).\nborder(sa, q). border(sa, nsw). border(sa, v). border(q, nsw). border(nsw, v).\ncolour(red; green; blue).\n\n1 { paint(R, C) : colour(C) } 1 :- region(R).\n:- border(A, B), paint(A, C), paint(B, C).\n\n#show paint/2.\n",
  time_limit: 10
)
```

In **SMT-LIB**, each region is an integer from 1 to 3, and neighbours are `distinct`.
z3 answers `sat` and the model table gives each region's number. Expected:
**Satisfiable**. This cell needs `z3` on `PATH` or set in the Settings cell; choose
*cvc5* in the engine select to use cvc5 instead.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Smtlib.Engines.Z3,
  "(set-logic QF_LIA)\n(declare-const wa Int) (declare-const nt Int) (declare-const sa Int) (declare-const q Int)\n(declare-const nsw Int) (declare-const v Int) (declare-const t Int)\n(assert (and (<= 1 wa 3) (<= 1 nt 3) (<= 1 sa 3) (<= 1 q 3) (<= 1 nsw 3) (<= 1 v 3) (<= 1 t 3)))\n(assert (distinct wa nt)) (assert (distinct wa sa)) (assert (distinct nt sa))\n(assert (distinct nt q)) (assert (distinct sa q)) (assert (distinct sa nsw))\n(assert (distinct sa v)) (assert (distinct q nsw)) (assert (distinct nsw v))\n(check-sat)\n(get-model)\n",
  time_limit: 10
)
```

In **Prolog**, the colouring is a predicate and the question is a goal. The engine
consults the program and asks the goal; the table shows the bindings of its
variables, one row per answer. Expected: **true**, one row.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Prolog.Engines.Swipl,
  "colour(red). colour(green). colour(blue).\n\naustralia(WA, NT, SA, Q, NSW, V, T) :-\n    maplist(colour, [WA, NT, SA, Q, NSW, V, T]),\n    WA \\== NT, WA \\== SA, NT \\== SA, NT \\== Q, SA \\== Q,\n    SA \\== NSW, SA \\== V, Q \\== NSW, NSW \\== V.\n",
  goal: "australia(WA, NT, SA, Q, NSW, V, T)",
  answers: 1,
  time_limit: 10
)
```

Prolog answers are not SZS statuses, so the verdict is the toplevel's: `true` or
`false`. A timeout is still SZS **Timeout**.

## Rewriting: egglog

egglog saturates an e-graph with rewrite rules and extracts the cheapest equivalent
term. Here `x · 1 + 0` simplifies to `x`. Expected: **completed**, the extracted term
`(Var "x")` and, with *Show the e-graph* on, the e-graph: each box an e-class, holding
the e-nodes found equal.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Egglog.Engines.Egglog,
  "(datatype Math (Num i64) (Var String) (Add Math Math) (Mul Math Math))\n\n(rewrite (Add a b) (Add b a))\n(rewrite (Mul a b) (Mul b a))\n(rewrite (Add a (Num 0)) a)\n(rewrite (Mul a (Num 1)) a)\n(rewrite (Mul a (Add b c)) (Add (Mul a b) (Mul a c)))\n\n(let expr (Add (Mul (Var \"x\") (Num 1)) (Num 0)))\n(run 5)\n(extract expr)\n",
  graph: true
)
```

## Linting as you type

Every language has a linter, run as you type. The program below has two problems:
`Region` occurs only once in its clause (a warning, placed on the variable), and the
last clause is missing a parenthesis (an error). Evaluating it raises `Loge.Error`
naming the error; fix the clause and it runs.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJlbmdpbmUiOiJzd2lwbCIsImxhbmd1YWdlIjoicHJvbG9nIiwib3B0aW9ucyI6eyJhbnN3ZXJzIjowLCJnb2FsIjoicGFpbnQod2EsIEMpIiwidGltZV9saW1pdCI6MTB9LCJzb3VyY2UiOiJjb2xvdXIocmVkKS4gY29sb3VyKGdyZWVuKS5cblxucGFpbnQoUmVnaW9uLCBDKSA6LSBjb2xvdXIoQykuXG5wYWludCh3YSwgcmVkIDotIHRydWUuXG4iLCJ2YXJpYWJsZSI6IiJ9","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoLoge.Cell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
Loge.run!(
  Loge.Prolog.Engines.Swipl,
  "colour(red). colour(green).\n\npaint(Region, C) :- colour(C).\npaint(wa, red :- true.\n",
  goal: "paint(wa, C)",
  answers: 0,
  time_limit: 10
)
```

## Loge from Elixir

The smart cell is optional: everything it does is a function call. The registry knows
every installed language and engine:

```elixir
for language <- Loge.Registry.languages() do
  {language.id(), Enum.map(Loge.Registry.engines_for(language.id()), & &1.id())}
end
```

`Loge.lint/3` is what runs under the editor. Each diagnostic has a code, a severity,
a range in the source and a message:

```elixir
Loge.lint(Loge.Smtlib.Language, "(declare-const x)\n(check-sat)", [])
```

Problems can also be built as data. This builds the map-colouring puzzle as a
propositional formula, one variable per region and colour, for the DIMACS package.
Expected: `{21, 55}`, 21 variables and 55 clauses.

```elixir
regions = ~w(wa nt sa q nsw v t)a
borders = [wa: :nt, wa: :sa, nt: :sa, nt: :q, sa: :q, sa: :nsw, sa: :v, q: :nsw, nsw: :v]
colours = ~w(red green blue)a

variable = fn region, colour ->
  Enum.find_index(regions, &(&1 == region)) * length(colours) +
    Enum.find_index(colours, &(&1 == colour)) + 1
end

some_colour = for r <- regions, do: for(c <- colours, do: variable.(r, c))
one_colour = for r <- regions, c <- colours, d <- colours, c < d, do: [-variable.(r, c), -variable.(r, d)]
differ = for {a, b} <- borders, c <- colours, do: [-variable.(a, c), -variable.(b, c)]

cnf = %Loge.Dimacs.Cnf{
  variables: length(regions) * length(colours),
  clauses: some_colour ++ one_colour ++ differ
}

{cnf.variables, length(cnf.clauses)}
```

`Loge.run!/3` solves it, here with clasp asked for every model, and the models
decode back into paintings. Expected: `{:satisfiable, 18, [...]}`, Tasmania being free.

```elixir
outcome = Loge.run!(Loge.Dimacs.Engines.Clasp, cnf, models: 0)
{:models, models} = List.keyfind(outcome.views, :models, 0)

paintings =
  for model <- models do
    for literal <- model, literal > 0 do
      region = Enum.at(regions, div(literal - 1, length(colours)))
      {region, Enum.at(colours, rem(literal - 1, length(colours)))}
    end
  end

{outcome.szs, length(paintings), hd(paintings)}
```

The `Loge.Outcome` holds the SZS status, the raw output and the views the Result tab
draws; it renders as a cell's result does:

```elixir
outcome
```

## Where to go next

Each language has a notebook of its own beside this one (`tptp.livemd`,
`dimacs.livemd`, `asp.livemd`, `prolog.livemd`, `egglog.livemd`, `smtlib.livemd`),
with more cases per engine. A language or engine of your own is an Elixir module
implementing `Loge.Language` or `Loge.Engine`, registered with `Loge.register/1`;
the smart cells pick it up with no change.
